WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.140
"Friends" premiered on NBC nearly 30 years
ago, running for 10 seasons.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:11.041
In that time, quite a few guest stars, actors
with recurring roles, and one main cast member have died.

00:00:11.041 --> 00:00:12.301
Here's the full list.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:17.949
"Friends" fans were shocked on October 28,
2023, when news broke that Matthew Perry had

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:19.710
died at the age of 54.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.119
The cause has yet to be determined, but as
of the making of this video, he died in an

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:27.279
apparent drowning in his hot tub at his Los
Angeles home.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.560
He was best known for his role as Chandler
Bing, the quick-witted and sarcastic member

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.916
of the "Friends" core cast.

00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:36.750
When in doubt, Chandler could always be counted
on to deliver a clever quip.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.952
"Could I interest you in a sarcastic comment?"

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:43.239
Over the course of the show, Chandler experienced
some of the best character development out

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:44.570
of all six of the friends.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.129
In the beginning, he was the embodiment of
a noncommittal man-child and corporate hanger-on.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:53.859
By the time he began his relationship with
Monica at the end of Season 4 and into Season

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:57.331
5, he showed signs of maturity and responsibility.

00:00:57.331 --> 00:01:02.620
The two marry in Season 7, Episode 23, "The
One With Monica And Chandler's Wedding," and

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.869
welcome a pair of twins in the series finale,
"The Last One."

00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:10.299
Perry continued his career in television with
prominent roles in Aaron Sorkin's "Studio

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:14.020
60 on the Sunset Strip" and his own revival
of "The Odd Couple."

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.979
He also published his bestselling memoir "Friends,
Lovers, and the Big Terrible Thing," in which

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:21.150
he detailed years of addiction issues.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:26.090
If "Friends" had a seventh friend, or a character
most familiar to even casual viewers beyond

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.200
the main six, it was Gunther, the manager
of the gang's coffee shop hangout.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.700
He was often seen flitting around in the background
at Central Perk, waiting on customers and

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.000
pulling espresso shots while rocking a tie,
a loud shirt, and tightly-cropped bleached-blond hair.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.830
On the occasion that Gunther spoke in one
of his more than 150 appearances, it was to

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:50.560
quietly and forlornly express his unrequited
love for former coworker Rachel, or to deliver

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.229
a blistering barb against her on-again, off-again
boyfriend Ross.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:55.970
"When's your birthday?"

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.561
"May 5th, why?"

00:01:57.561 --> 00:01:59.840
"I'm just making a list of people's birthdays."

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.683
"Oh, mine's December--"

00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:02.650
"Yeah, whatever."

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:07.049
In real life, to help pay the bills between
gigs in addition to his continuing role as

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:11.918
Gunther, actor James Michael Tyler worked
at a Hollywood coffee shop called The Bourgeois Pig.

00:02:11.918 --> 00:02:13.963
He told BuzzFeed in 2014,

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:18.600
"I was one of their first baristas — I think
I started there in 1990 or so."

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.620
He appeared on the long-awaited, long-delayed
"Friends" reunion special in 2021, but via

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.519
Zoom because of health concerns.

00:02:25.519 --> 00:02:30.410
Tyler told "Today" that he'd been diagnosed
with advanced prostate cancer in 2018, and

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.240
the disease had spread to his bones.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.629
In October 2021, the 59-year-old actor died.

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:38.989
Jennifer Aniston, who played Rachel, wrote
in an Instagram post.

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:41.060
"'Friends' would not have been the same without
you.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.040
Thank you for the laughter you brought to
the show and to all of our lives.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.483
You will be so missed."

00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:50.720
Rachel's overbearing father Dr. Leonard Green
may only appear in four "Friends" episodes,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.810
but he casts a huge shadow over the show,
especially Rachel's character.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.500
Dr. Green was extremely well-to-do in his
career but quite hands-off with his children,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.920
and as a result his three daughters have become
spoiled brats who have no idea how the world

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:04.970
works at all.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.550
When we finally meet gruff Dr. Green in season
2's "The One With the Two Parties," so much

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.690
about Rachel's upbringing and current neuroses
becomes clear.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.090
We also have some moments of empathy for Rachel,
who is desperately trying to break out of

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:22.269
the old entitled patterns that no longer serve
her as a wannabe self-sufficient career woman.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:26.790
While Dr. Green was a character we loved to
hate, we all could agree with him on one thing:

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.267
Ross is a jerk and Rachel could do better.

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:33.840
Actor Ron Leibman had a long and distinguished
career across movies, television, and the

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.010
stage that included a Tony Award for his work
in "Angels in America."

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:44.250
Leibman passed away in December of 2019 of
pneumonia complications at the age of 82.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:49.230
Mr. Treeger was the gruff, no-nonsense superintendent
of the New York City apartment building in

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:54.540
which Monica, Rachel, Chandler, and Joey resided
on "Friends," and he factored into the plots

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.730
of five episodes between 1995 and 2001.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:04.030
Probably the most memorable occasion came
in the 1997 installment "The One with the Ballroom Dancing."

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:08.600
After catching Rachel stuffing the trash chute
and making her cry, Mr. Treeger decides to

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.019
evict her and Monica from the building, leading
Joey to stick up for his friends and agreeing

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:17.660
to be the super's ballroom dance practice
partner at a superintendents' ball.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.440
Portraying Mr. Treeger was the prolific and
highly recognizable character actor Mike Hagerty,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.509
known for his mustache, his Chicago accent,
and his tendency to play working class characters.

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:31.720
Before "Friends," he played a cable TV station
employee in "Wayne's World" and the best friend

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:32.362
in "Overboard."

00:04:32.362 --> 00:04:37.301
After "Friends," he appeared in "Brooklyn
Nine-Nine," "Shameless," and, in 2022, he

00:04:37.301 --> 00:04:40.430
was part of the main cast of HBO's "Somebody
Somewhere."

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:45.770
The star of that show, Bridget Everett, announced
on Instagram that Hagerty had died on May

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.443
5, 2022, writing,

00:04:47.443 --> 00:04:51.820
"A beloved character actor, his love of his
hometown of Chicago and his family were the

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.400
cornerstones of his life."

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.370
According to TMZ, Hagerty suffered a negative
reaction to an antibiotic used to treat a

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.010
leg infection, and he fell into a coma.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.680
The actor was 67 years old.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.880
Before Gunther, for a brief moment in "Friends"
history, there was Terry, the surly coffee

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.910
shop owner whose tongue was as sharp as his
black coffee.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:15.150
While only on screen for two episodes, Terry
offered one of the most cruel descriptions

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.500
of Phoebe's music.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.370
It's so dreadfully mean it somehow comes all
the way back around to funny:

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:29.130
"She makes me want to put my finger through
my eye into my brain and swirl it around."

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.500
It's also courtesy of Terry that we have one
of the show's most memorable cameos, as Chrissie

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.060
Hynde from the Pretenders plays an open mic
set at the café.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.569
Eventually, Phoebe even teaches her the chorus
of her infamous ditty "Smelly Cat."

00:05:41.569 --> 00:05:45.819
While actor Max Wright might be better known
for his turn as Mr. Tanner on "ALF," his mark

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:47.867
on "Friends" as Terry is indelible.

00:05:47.867 --> 00:05:53.686
Wright passed away at the age of 75 in June
2019 after a long battle with cancer.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:58.260
A key part of Monica's character arc finds
her chasing her lifelong dream of being a

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.090
chef, and on her difficult road down that
path she writes a review of Allesandro's Italian

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.120
restaurant for the Chelsea Reporter.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.590
The review absolutely eviscerates the establishment
to such a degree that its owner, Allesandro,

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.260
actually comes to Monica's apartment to chew
her out.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.220
While there, Monica learns that the reason
the food was so bad was because Allesandro

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.910
doesn't actually know how to cook.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:22.150
Also, he's not even Italian — he's Lebanese.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:26.530
After tasting Monica's food, a begrudging
Allesandro invites Monica to be his new head

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:31.250
chef, which turns into another huge conflict
as the old head chef was literally related

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.590
to everyone else on staff, so their anger
turns to her.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.720
She concocts shenanigans with Joey to get
her new staff to take her seriously.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.930
Actor Taylor Negron, who memorably brought
Allesandro to life, was diagnosed with liver

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:49.287
cancer in 2008 and succumbed to the illness
in January 2015 at the age of 57.

00:06:49.287 --> 00:06:55.260
Ross Gellar's relationships are so dysfunctional
he's on a first name basis with his divorce attorney, Russell.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:00.660
Russell is constantly disturbed by Ross' compulsive
behavior, but he also recognizes a cash cow

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.000
when he sees one, calling Ross one of his
favorite clients thanks to all the business.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.430
Ross got divorced not once, but twice in a
year, first from Emily Waltham after saying

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.780
Rachel's name at the altar, and then later
from Rachel after a blackout drunk drive-through

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.410
Elvis wedding in Vegas.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:19.940
Russell is the one to give Rachel the unfortunate
news that Ross didn't actually file the annulment

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.861
papers since he didn't want to be divorced
three times.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:24.980
"Ross!

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.541
Are you crazy?

00:07:26.541 --> 00:07:28.180
I'm still your wife?"

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:33.060
The moment has big consequences for the entire
gang, not just Rachel and Ross.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.180
Played by Ron Glass — who was better known
for his roles on "Barney Miller" and "Firefly"

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.470
— Russell was a rare voice of reason on
"Friends."

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:44.759
Glass passed away in November 2016 of undisclosed
causes at the age of 71.

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:49.289
"Friends" didn't often go into dream territory,
rarely exploring alternate avenues for the

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:52.479
characters or offering fresh perspectives
on the past and future.

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:56.690
But thankfully, fans do have the two-part
"The One That Could Have Been," which dives

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:01.319
into an alternate universe in which Rachel
married Barry, Monica never lost all that

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:06.040
weight, Joey stayed on at "Days of Our Lives,"
Chandler is an aspiring comedian, Ross and

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:10.310
Carol remain unhappily married, and in the
biggest twist of all, Phoebe is an investment

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:15.524
banker who chain smokes rather than a floopy
vegetarian masseuse with a unique fashion sense.

00:08:15.524 --> 00:08:20.009
It's in Phoebe's portion of the story that
Paul Gleason, perhaps best known as Vice Principal

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:23.240
Vernon from "The Breakfast Club," appears
as her boss, Jack.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.940
He's concerned after Phoebe loses millions
of dollars on a bad deal and has a heart attack.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:34.310
Gleason passed away in May 2006 at the age
of 67 after struggling with mesothelioma and

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:38.390
lung cancer complications caused by asbestos
exposure.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:42.349
One of the other narrative tricks that kept
things lively on "Friends" was the overwhelming

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:47.120
number of celebrity cameos, with A-listers
often playing love interests for the gang.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.380
One of the rare non-romantic cameos comes
courtesy of Robin Williams and Billy Crystal

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:55.060
as Thomas and Tim in "The One With the Ultimate
Fighting Champion."

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:59.490
Thomas and Tim make their way onto the gang's
couch in Central Perk on a particularly busy

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.324
morning as a hysterical Thomas tries to figure
out who might be having an affair with his wife.

00:09:04.324 --> 00:09:08.940
As it turns out, the culprit is Tim himself,
which unfolds in dialogue that was improvised

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.440
by Williams and Crystal on set, according
to Screen Rant.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.970
"You are no longer my friend!

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:15.990
We are finished!"

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:20.860
The gang is in awe, and rightfully so, watching
these two comedic masters at work.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:26.450
Heartbreakingly, Robin Williams died by suicide
on August 11, 2014, after a long struggle

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.160
with a number of physical and mental health
issues.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.910
He was 63.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.800
Ross Gellar famously had a pet monkey during
the first season of "Friends."

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.900
Marcel is a capuchin who starts off sweet,
but quickly grows more and more aggressive

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:43.290
as he ages, leading to an uncomfortable adulthood
in which he cannot stop humping everything

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.300
around him, including Rachel's childhood stuffed
animals.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:50.620
As his friends get more and more annoyed with
Marcel's disruptions, Ross decides to let

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.149
the Bronx Zoo adopt him.

00:09:52.149 --> 00:09:56.710
But when he goes to visit, he finds out from
shifty zoo administrator Dean Lipson that

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:57.760
Marcel has died.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:58.790
Or has he?

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:02.870
The intrigue builds when a janitor tells Ross
that Marcel has been shipped out to California

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:07.070
to become an actor in Hollywood, and the zoo
wants to keep the information under wraps.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:11.530
When Ross finds out that Marcel will be starring
in "Outbreak 2" filming locally, he's able

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:15.100
to say goodbye to his old friend with his
favorite song, "In the Jungle."

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.720
Comedy icon Fred Willard might only have been
on screen briefly in "Friends" as Lipson,

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.720
but he brought his characteristic snark to
the role in a way that played perfectly with

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:25.450
Ross' natural suspicion.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:30.220
Willard passed away in May 2020 of a sudden
heart attack at the age of 86, never having

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.130
retired from acting.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:36.620
In Season 4's "The One with Joey's Dirty Day,"
Joey lands another acting gig.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:41.010
This time it's a high-profile one, in a movie
starring the legendary Charlton Heston.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:45.579
Before he reports to the set, Joey takes Chandler
on a fishing trip, and upon returning, falls

00:10:45.579 --> 00:10:47.399
asleep while memorizing his lines.

00:10:47.399 --> 00:10:51.769
He doesn't get to take a shower before going
to work and smells terrible, so he sneaks

00:10:51.769 --> 00:10:54.839
into the only dressing room equipped with
a shower — Heston's.

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:59.130
The seasoned professional discovers Joey,
writes off his misbehavior to nerves, and

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.079
warns him not to cross him again.

00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:06.080
"Put some pants on, kid, so I can kick your
butt."

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:11.510
An icon of epic biblical films in the 1950s,
Heston starred as Moses in "The Ten Commandments"

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:15.510
and the title character in "Ben-Hur," which
won him an Academy Award.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:21.079
In the '60s and '70s, he shifted to headlining
big-budget dystopian sci-fi movies, including

00:11:21.079 --> 00:11:24.279
"The Omega Man," "Planet of the Apes," and
"Soylent Green."

00:11:24.279 --> 00:11:29.200
Heston's family told CNN that the actor died
at home in Beverly Hills on April 5, 2008,

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:34.320
at his home, not long after entering the latter
stages of Alzheimer's disease.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.642
Heston was 84.

00:11:35.642 --> 00:11:40.300
Joey often talked about his large Italian
family and his grandmothers in particular.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.360
Season 5's "The One Where Ross Can't Flirt"
introduces one of them, Nonnie.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.386
Joey invites her over to see him on an episode
of "Law and Order."

00:11:47.386 --> 00:11:52.230
However, his scenes ended up getting cut,
so he scrambles to film a fake "Law and Order"

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:56.310
episode to trick his Nonnie, who reportedly
doesn't know any English other than the name

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.681
"Sam Waterston" and enough to eavesdrop on
an embarrassing conversation involving Ross.

00:12:00.781 --> 00:12:03.339
French actress Lilyan Chauvin played Nonnie.

00:12:03.339 --> 00:12:07.190
In the years before that appearance, she regularly
featured in "The Young and the Restless,"

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.820
"Days of Our Lives," and "Falcon Crest."

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:14.279
She also hosted and produced the business
cable TV talk show, "Hollywood Structured."

00:12:14.279 --> 00:12:19.018
After "Friends," Chauvin guest-starred on
"Ugly Betty," "Frasier," and "Malcolm in the Middle."

00:12:19.018 --> 00:12:24.880
According to Chauvin's Los Angeles Times obituary,
the actor died in June 2008, four decades

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:28.715
after a breast cancer diagnosis, due to congestive
heart disease.

00:12:28.715 --> 00:12:30.000
She was 82.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.310
The same person played two very different
roles in back-to-back seasons of "Friends."

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:38.829
In the Season 6 episode, "The One with Rachel's
Sister," Alexis Arquette plays a customer

00:12:38.829 --> 00:12:39.875
at Central Perk.

00:12:39.875 --> 00:12:44.320
And then in Season 7's "The One with Chandler's
Dad," Arquette returns as a server at the

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.399
Las Vegas drag club where Chandler's transgender
parent performs.

00:12:48.399 --> 00:12:53.100
Both episodes make clear reference to family,
and Alexis was part of the prominent acting

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:57.630
Arquette famliy, along with siblings Patricia,
Rosanna, and David.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:01.680
David was a fellow "Friends" guest, and once
husband of Courteney Cox.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.779
A transwoman, Alexis documented her transition
in "Alexis Arquette: She's My Brother."

00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:11.769
Arquette was best known for small but important
parts in two major '90s movies: the reluctant

00:13:11.769 --> 00:13:16.350
criminal who unloads a gun at John Travolta
and Samuel L. Jackson's hitmen in "Pulp Fiction,"

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:20.000
and the Boy George-obsessed keyboardist in
the wedding band in "The Wedding Singer."

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:26.660
Arquette died in September 2016 at age 47
due to a bacterial infection and cardiac event

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:28.621
complicated by HIV.

00:13:28.621 --> 00:13:33.220
Stan Kirsch was only on one episode of "Friends,"
but it was a very memorable one that would

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.970
possibly cause some controversy if it aired
today.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.790
In "The One With the Ick Factor," Monica goes
to a college party and pretends to be 22 instead

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:42.460
of her actual age of 26.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:47.210
She meets a cute guy named Ethan who says
he's a senior, and Monica agrees to go out

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:51.019
with him even under her false pretenses, thinking
he's in college.

00:13:51.019 --> 00:13:55.259
After the two sleep together, Monica finds
out that Ethan is really a senior in high

00:13:55.259 --> 00:14:00.320
school, and only 17 to boot, making this not
just an "ick factor," but an actual crime

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:01.643
in some places.

00:14:01.643 --> 00:14:05.502
"My lie didn't make one of us a felon in 48
states!"

00:14:05.502 --> 00:14:09.339
Kirsch, better known for his long run on the
"Highlander" television series, played Ethan.

00:14:09.339 --> 00:14:14.160
He died by suicide in January of 2020 at the
age of 51.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:18.720
Iris is Monica's aunt who shows up in the
Season 1 episode, "The One with All the Poker."

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.839
She is a bit abrasive and rough around the
edges, which is probably why Monica seeks

00:14:22.839 --> 00:14:26.480
her assistance in helping her and her friends
learn how to play poker.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:30.660
Aunt Iris is apparently an old card sharp
who's been playing poker since early childhood.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:35.342
She agrees to help, but is rude, impatient,
and claims to have hit Tony Randall with a

00:14:35.342 --> 00:14:37.888
car — but that was just a lesson in bluffing.

00:14:37.888 --> 00:14:43.146
Beverly Garland played the sketchy aunt, one
of nearly 200 roles in a nearly 60-year career.

00:14:43.146 --> 00:14:43.646
"Really?"

00:14:43.646 --> 00:14:44.146
"No!

00:14:44.146 --> 00:14:44.646
That's bluffing!

00:14:44.696 --> 00:14:46.259
Lesson number one!"

00:14:46.259 --> 00:14:50.680
She started off in low-budget movies like
"It Conquered the World" and "Swamp Women,"

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:55.769
then portrayed Barbara Harper Douglas in "My
Three Sons," one of the dozens of sitcom parts.

00:14:55.769 --> 00:15:00.709
In December 2008, the Los Angeles Times reported
that Garland died at her home in the Hollywood

00:15:00.709 --> 00:15:03.269
Hills following a long period of illness.

00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:05.000
Garland was 82.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:09.430
Phoebe's adoptive grandmother Frances only
appears in one "Friends" episode, but it's

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:12.850
a very significant one for the most eccentric
friend of the bunch.

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:16.240
Pheebs didn't actually know that she and her
sister had been adopted at all.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:20.529
All the photographs of her "father" around
Frances' house were actually the placeholder

00:15:20.529 --> 00:15:24.680
images that came with the picture frames,
explaining why Phoebe insists for the rest

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.080
of the series that her grandmother is in hell.

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:32.267
Thankfully, though, before Frances' death,
she guides Phoebe to her actual father, Frank Buffay.

00:15:32.267 --> 00:15:37.060
Phoebe also finds out about her brother, Frank
Jr., which sets up many new character arcs

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:41.050
for the most eccentric of the Friends and
her even stranger family, like the time when

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:44.180
she carries her brother and his much older
wife's triplets.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:48.959
Frances was played by Audra Lindley, iconic
for her role as Mrs. Roper on "Three's Company."

00:15:48.959 --> 00:15:55.040
She died in October of 1997 after a long struggle
with leukemia, meaning she never had a chance

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:59.589
to see how her small appearance on the show
would in fact change pop culture history.

00:15:59.589 --> 00:16:05.139
Sadly, too many "Friends" guest stars have
passed away to discuss them all in full in this video.

00:16:05.139 --> 00:16:06.139
The others include:

00:16:06.139 --> 00:16:08.089
Gary Collins, who played himself

00:16:08.089 --> 00:16:11.020
Conchata Ferrell, who played a judge in Season
6

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:13.930
Joel Beeson, who played Todd the Hombre Man

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:16.920
Mary Pat Gleason, who played Nurse Sizemore

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.410
Shelley Berman, who played Mr. Kaplan

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.990
Peter Dennis, who played Sherman Whitfield

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.130
Kellie Waymire, who played Colleen

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:26.850
Gretchen Wyler, who played Mrs. Burkart

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.350
Phil Leeds, who played Mr. Adelman

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.259
Alaina Reed Hall, who played The Admissions
Woman

00:16:32.259 --> 00:16:33.940
Richard Roat, who played Burt

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:36.829
Danny Dayton, who played Buddy Doyle

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:39.160
Paxton Whitehead, who played Mr. Waltham

